{"findings": [{"file": "src/completion_queue.zig", "line": 381, "severity": "major", "category": "correctness", "title": "isClosed() cannot separate a real drained close from a spurious win, and its doc tells the driver to stop draining", "body": "The doc comment offers `isClosed()` as the discriminator a select driver uses on `error.Closed`: false means spurious (re-poll), true means "a real close (stop draining)". Only the false direction holds. `close()` is documented (line 146) to leave in-flight operations running and still delivered, so `closed == true` does NOT mean drained; the drained predicate is `closed and pending.isEmpty()`, exactly what `wait` (line 185) and `isReady` (line 328) compute under one lock.

Mechanism: a stale wake of the kind `getResult` describes (line 309) can win the queue arm at any time, including while the queue is closed with operations still in flight. `getResult` then returns `error.Closed` although `completed` is empty and `pending` is not. The driver asks `isClosed()`, gets true, and follows the doc: it stops draining. Every in-flight operation is abandoned. For the heap-backed submissions the module header mandates for a multi-task queue (line 14), those completions are never handed out by `next`, so their memory and results leak. The same misread happens on a genuine close that races the driver's re-poll.

A caller cannot repair this by composing the existing accessors: `isClosed()` then `hasPending()` are two separate lock acquisitions, and an operation moving from `pending` to `completed` between them reads as "closed and nothing pending" while a result is sitting in `completed`, so the driver drops it. The predicate has to be evaluated under a single lock. The primitive to export is the drained state that `isReady` already computes, not `closed` alone."}, {"file": "src/completion_queue.zig", "line": 386, "severity": "minor", "category": "tests", "title": "New public isClosed() has no test and no caller", "body": "`isClosed` is new public API on a module whose every other public entry point is covered by a test in this file, including the close/drain distinction it claims to serve (`close hands out in-flight completions before error.Closed`, line 986; `select on a drained queue reports error.Closed`, line 1216). Nothing in the tree calls `isClosed`, and no test pins the contract the doc states. The behaviour the comment promises for the select driver is therefore unexercised: the case it exists for (a queue that is closed while operations are still in flight, versus one that is open) is never observed through this function. A test that closes a queue over an in-flight timer and asserts what a driver may conclude at that moment would have shown that `isClosed() == true` does not license the driver to stop draining."}]}
